<section class="bg-white py-16"><div class="container-page space-y-12">
<Section id="interpretation" index="01" eyebrow="Interpretation" title="Muller v King's College Hospital NHS Foundation Trust [2017] EWHC 128 (QB), [2017] QB 987">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: perceptual error in image interpretation.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: missed malignant melanoma on foot imaging with later expert review showing a visible lesion.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: Bolam applies to judgment. It does not protect failure to see what is plainly visible to a competent radiologist. Peer evidence that others might also have missed it does not cure a perceptual miss of a demonstrable abnormality.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> Muller is the most-cited modern authority for the proposition that Bolam governs <em>judgment</em> but not <em>perception</em>. It is the case to put alongside Penney (index 02) whenever a missed lesion was visible in retrospect on archived images.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> the defence that "many would have missed it" fails if the lesion was demonstrably there. Protect the report instead: systematic search, comparison with priors, and honest expression of uncertainty where the study is limited.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> Muller defeats the "pure diagnosis" shortcut in the claimant's favour too — visibility must be proved as fact, with the original images, before Bolam drops away. Instruct the expert to address demonstrability first.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="screening" index="02" eyebrow="Screening" title="Penney, Palmer and Cannon v East Kent HA [1999] Lloyd's Rep Med 123; aff'd [2000] Lloyd's Rep Med 41 (CA)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: standard of care in screening imaging and cytology — when <em>Bolam</em> does not apply. Commonly cited as <em>Penny v East Kent Health Authority</em>.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: three false-negative cervical screening reports. Cytopathologists and screeners classified slides showing precancerous cells as negative. The judge found the abnormalities were plainly visible on the slides.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: first question is factual — did the slide show abnormalities. If present, failure to report or refer fell below standard. Evidence that others might have missed it was not determinative. <em>Buchan, Clinical Negligence: A Practical Guide</em> (8th ed, Ch 6) treats this as the leading example of the Bolam / Bolitho boundary: where there is no genuine exercise of skill and judgment to defend — a perceptual miss of what is demonstrably there — Bolam does not protect the defendant. The Court of Appeal affirmed. The same analysis applies in radiology to a plainly visible lesion missed on a radiograph or slide: see <em>Muller</em> (index 01) and <em>EXP v Barker</em> (index 07). UK and Scots law alike: the Scottish root of Bolam is <em>Hunter v Hanley 1955 SC 200</em>, applied uniformly across both jurisdictions.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> screening and high-volume plain-film work live or die on perceptual vigilance. The case separates <em>misses</em> (nothing to defend under Bolam) from <em>judgment calls</em> (defensible). Double reading, checklists and protected reporting time directly answer this risk.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> if the lesion was there to see, peer evidence that others might have missed it will not save the report. Record what was reviewed, compare with priors, and state uncertainty rather than issuing a flat negative where doubt exists.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> plead and prove the factual premise first — was the abnormality demonstrably present on the archived images? Sequence expert evidence accordingly: visibility before opinion. Penney is the authority to cite when the defence runs Bolam against a plain perceptual miss.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="task-standard" index="03" eyebrow="Task standard" title="FB v Rana [2017] EWCA Civ 334 (claim against Princess Alexandra Hospital NHS Trust)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: standard for junior staff deciding whether to order — and how to interpret — diagnostic imaging.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: a deteriorating child with atypical symptoms. The junior doctor failed to take an adequate history and failed to order indicated brain and chest imaging, delaying diagnosis and causing permanent brain damage.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: standard is set by post and task, not by seniority or personal inexperience. A junior occupying an ED post who interprets a radiograph — or decides whether imaging is indicated — is judged against a reasonably competent practitioner doing that task. Failure to order imaging the post required was breach. <em>Buchan</em> (Ch 6) confirms: inexperience is no defence. RCR Standards (3rd ed, October 2025 — <em>ppqi_reporting-standards-guidance.pdf</em> extract) reinforce the corollary: non-radiology doctors and non-medical practitioners working beyond their imaging competence must have an agreement for radiology review, and no employee should work beyond competence or without adequate rest. Same rule in Scotland: <em>Hunter v Hanley 1955 SC 200</em> states the standard by reference to the ordinary competent practitioner in the relevant role.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> most out-of-hours plain films are first read by non-radiologists. FB is the authority that holds that first read to the standard of the post, so ED and referral pathways must define who may issue a provisional read and when a formal radiology review is mandatory.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> you own the safety net — agree written review arrangements for examinations reported outside radiology, audit provisional reads, and refuse service models that leave juniors interpreting beyond competence without cover.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> do not discount a claim because the reader was junior. Frame breach against the occupant-of-the-post standard and test the Trust's supervision and review system alongside the individual miss.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="communication" index="04" eyebrow="Communication" title="Gwyn v South Tyneside and Sunderland NHS Foundation Trust [2021] (unreported — no neutral citation; verify before reliance)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: fail-safe reporting of critical findings.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: incidental nodule suspicious for malignancy reported but not directly communicated. No alert reached the treating team.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: duty extends beyond interpretation to effective communication. A report filed silently on an electronic system does not discharge duty where urgent action is required. Closed loop notification is required.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> Gwyn is the fail-safe case: the error was not perception but transmission — an abnormal result filed where nobody acted on it. It converts "I reported it" from a complete defence into the start of the duty: the result must reach someone who will act.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> design reports for acknowledgement — critical and unexpected findings need direct communication, documented receipt, and escalation where acknowledgement fails. The RCR Standards' acknowledgement and addendum procedures exist precisely for this claim pattern.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> Gwyn claims are proved through IT and administrative records — filing logs, acknowledgement audits, inbox cover — rather than image review. Disclosure should target the communication chain end to end.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="consent" index="05" eyebrow="Consent" title="Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] UKSC 11, [2015] AC 1430">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: informed consent for imaging and interventional procedures.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: material delivery risks not disclosed.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: disclosure judged by reasonable patient standard. For embolisation, biopsy and angioplasty, discuss material risks, complications and alternatives specific to that patient. Record the discussion.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> Montgomery governs every image-guided procedure — biopsy, embolisation, angioplasty, injection — because the Montgomery duty attaches to the decision to intervene, and in radiology that decision is often the radiologist's. The MDU's Achilles-tendon case (damages £12,500, costs near five times that) shows undocumented verbal warnings settle claims even where the procedure itself was reasonable.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> consent is a documented dialogue, not a form: record the material risks discussed (including a ~1:1,000 rupture risk where relevant), the alternatives including doing nothing, and what this particular patient weighed. IR(ME)R justification and Montgomery disclosure should be recorded together.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> Montgomery claims turn on the consent record and on what a reasonable person in <em>this</em> patient's position would have wanted to know. Absence of documentation is routinely treated as absence of warning — subpoena clinic letters, lists and ultrasound report text, not just the signed form.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="incidental-findings" index="06" eyebrow="Incidental findings" title="Halligan et al. — Incidental Pathology in Abdominal Radiology Claims (2025–2026)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: what standard applies when the allegation concerns pathology incidental to the primary clinical question?</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: a retrospective review of 112 medicolegal cases by five expert abdominal radiologists (Halligan, Blunt, Boone, Higginson, Plumb) published in <em>Clinical Radiology</em> 92 (2026) 107162. Of 176 imaging examinations reviewed (56% CT, 20% MRI), 41 (37%) involved allegations citing failure to report incidental pathology. In 29 of these 41 cases (71%), the experts judged the interpretation as reasonable — not substandard. In the remaining 12 cases (29%), the interpretation was judged substandard. Across the entire cohort, the most common allegation was "primary tumour unreported" (40 cases, 36%), followed by "perforation unreported" (12 cases, 11%).</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: claimants routinely cite the RCR Standards for Interpretation and Reporting to support allegations that incidental pathology should have been detected. However, the authors — all acting as expert witnesses for both claimant and defendant — found that the Bolam test remains the proper framework. Where clinical details direct attention to a specific question (e.g. "abdominal pain ? cause" on an unprepared abdominal CT), failure to report a small incidental colonic tumour was often judged reasonable. The RCR 2025 Standards' recognition that "interpretation may be incorrect if referral details are deficient, absent or misleading" provides some protective recognition of this reality. The case is cited both as evidence of claimant strategy and as expert consensus that the incidentaloma question requires nuanced Bolam application rather than a "pure diagnosis" approach.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> Halligan quantifies the incidentaloma litigation burden — 37% of abdominal claims — and rebuts the assumption that every missed incidental is negligence: 71% of challenged incidental interpretations were judged reasonable. It is the empirical backbone for defending inconspicuous misses made without a clinical steer.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> the protective factors are concrete — adequate referral information, attention-directing clinical details, and documented search of the relevant anatomy. Where referrals are vague ("abdominal pain ? cause"), say what the study can and cannot exclude, and push back on referral quality through governance channels.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> use Halligan to calibrate settlement — most incidental allegations fail on breach — but check which cohort the case falls in: missed tumour forming 56% of incidental allegations faces different expert dynamics from a small incidental lesion on an unprepared study. The RCR Standards citation cuts both ways and must be read with the 2025 referral-quality wording.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="missed-aneurysm" index="07" eyebrow="Missed aneurysm" title="EXP v Barker [2017] EWCA Civ 63">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: standard of care for diagnostic interpretation under the Bolam test and the weighting of expert witness testimony.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: a consultant neuroradiologist interpreted an MRI for severe headaches and missed a right middle cerebral artery aneurysm; twelve years later it ruptured, leaving permanent catastrophic brain damage.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: a radiologist is accountable where a reasonably competent specialist ought to have detected the abnormality, even if a defence expert argues the error was reasonable. The Court of Appeal upheld the High Court finding of negligence.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> aneurysm on MRA/MRI is the paradigm "demonstrable but missed" lesion — small, adjacent to vessel, and fatal if ruptured. EXP shows that a single missed aneurysm can sustain a catastrophic claim even against a consultant neuroradiologist.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> vascular review needs its own pass — source images as well as reconstructions, documented aneurysm search, and a low threshold for second opinion or addendum where headache imaging is equivocal.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> EXP pairs naturally with Muller and Penney: where the aneurysm is demonstrable on the archived study, defence expert evidence of reasonableness carries little weight. Fix the visibility fact early, then address limitation — here twelve years elapsed before rupture.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="misinterpretation" index="08" eyebrow="Misinterpretation" title="Brady v Southend University Hospital NHS Foundation Trust [2020] EWHC 289 (QB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: whether an incorrect diagnostic interpretation on imaging automatically constitutes negligence.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: radiologists reporting CT scans identified an abdominal mass as omental infarction rather than severe infection, delaying treatment.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: an incorrect opinion is not automatically negligent where the reasoning was supported by a responsible body of medical opinion (Bolam/Bolitho) and expressed with appropriate caution. Claim dismissed.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> abdominal CT forces genuine differential diagnoses — infarct versus infection, ischaemia versus inflammation — where reasonable radiologists differ. Brady is the counterweight to Muller and Penney: it protects honest, reasoned interpretation from hindsight.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> the protection depends on the report showing its working — differential stated, uncertainty expressed, and follow-up or escalation advised. A bare, confident wrong answer earns no Bolam shelter; a reasoned, cautious one may.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> test Brady claims on the reasoning, not the outcome. If the report documents a logical differential supported by contemporaneous findings, the claimant must go further and show the reasoning itself was illogical (Bolitho) — outcome error alone is insufficient.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="subjective-variance" index="09" eyebrow="Subjective variance" title="Spellman v Portsmouth Hospitals University NHS Trust [2024] EWHC 2011 (KB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: standard of interpretation in a "pure diagnosis" radiology claim — when disagreement between radiologists is negligence and when it is not.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: after a fall with severe back pain, the claimant alleged the emergency MRI showed acute cauda equina syndrome (CES) compression which the reporting radiologist missed, reporting only mild spinal stenosis. Sweeting J heard competing expert evidence and preferred the defendant's evidence that acute compression was factually absent from the scan as acquired.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: subjective variance in reporting is acceptable where the evaluation is logically supported (<em>Bolam</em> as qualified by <em>Bolitho v City and Hackney HA [1998] AC 232</em>). The court rejected the "pure diagnosis" invitation to decide negligence simply by preferring one expert's reading of the images: the question remains whether the defendant's interpretation was supported by a responsible, logical body of radiological opinion at the time. Here it was, and the claim was dismissed. Contrast <em>Penney</em> (index 02) and <em>EXP v Barker</em> (index 07): where an abnormality is demonstrably present and plainly visible, Bolam does not save a perceptual miss. <em>Halligan et al. (Clin Radiol 2026)</em> — reviewed from the <em>medicolegalcourtcase.pdf</em> extract — shows why this boundary matters in practice: most challenged interpretations (71% of incidental, 68% of non-incidental in that series) were judged reasonable. The Scottish test is identical: <em>Hunter v Hanley 1955 SC 200</em> (the Scots origin of <em>Bolam</em>) and <em>Bolitho</em> apply in the Court of Session as in England and Wales. MDU analysis (<em>article-1789575458816.pdf</em> extract: 200 claims, ~75% defended) confirms that reasonable interpretation in light of the clinical information provided remains defensible.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> emergency spinal MRI is the highest-stakes "pure diagnosis" examination in general radiology. Spellman confirms that a careful, reasoned negative — documented sequences, levels examined, and why criteria for acute compression were not met — is defensible even when a later expert disagrees.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> show your reasoning on the face of the report. "No acute compression" should be supported by what was assessed (conus, cauda equina, canal, discs, haematoma) so a Bolitho challenge has something logical to test. Escalate clinically where the picture is borderline rather than hedging in the text alone.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> a battle of experts on the images is not enough — the claimant must show no responsible, logical body of radiologists would have reported as the defendant did. Ask experts to address the scan <em>as acquired</em> and to separate hindsight from contemporaneous reasonableness.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="subtle-ct-signs" index="10" eyebrow="Subtle CT signs" title="White v Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust [2011] EWHC 3822 (QB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: diagnostic threshold for subtle visceral injury on CT imaging — what a competent consultant radiologist must spot.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: an abdominal CT on a trauma patient was reported as normal, missing early signs of bowel perforation (subtle extraluminal gas) that led to peritonitis and severe sepsis.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: judgment for the claimant. Expert evidence established that while subtle, the signs of extraluminal gas were present on the study and should have been identified by a reasonably competent consultant radiologist. This is the <em>Penney</em> side of the line (index 02): a factual miss of a demonstrable abnormality, not a defensible difference of opinion as in <em>Brady</em> (index 08) or <em>Spellman</em> (index 09). MPS analysis (<em>MPS medicolegal.txt</em> extract: highest reporting-error payment above £400,000, 2016–mid-2023) lists missed anastomotic leak and bowel perforation among the recurring reporting-error allegations. Practical standard: windowing, multiplanar review and explicit comment on extraluminal gas in trauma CT, per RCR reporting standards.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> trauma CT is reported fast, often overnight, where subtle extraluminal gas is the classic "present but missed" finding. White makes lung-window and multiplanar review of bowel and mesentery a litigation-tested standard, not a preference.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> build the negative into the report — state the windows and planes reviewed and that no extraluminal gas or leak was identified. If the study is degraded or the patient unstable, say so and advise what follow-up closes the gap.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> White is a visibility case masquerading as subtlety — obtain windowed, multiplanar reconstructions for the expert, and test whether the "subtle" sign was in fact demonstrable to a competent consultant at the time.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="whole-image" index="11" eyebrow="Whole-image duty" title="KJY v University College London Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust [2023] EWHC 2719 (KB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: duty to examine the "whole picture" on a plain radiograph — including incidental pathology outside the referral question.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: an infant underwent abdominal X-rays for suspected bowel rotation. The gastrointestinal tract was non-pathological, but the films incidentally demonstrated hip malalignment which went unreported. Developmental dysplasia of the hips (DDH) was diagnosed at 14 months. The Trust admitted breach on the failure to report the hips.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: radiologists have a legal duty to review the entire image and report obvious incidental pathology even where it falls outside the referral question. The RCR Standards (3rd ed, October 2025 — reviewed from the <em>ppqi_reporting-standards-guidance.pdf</em> extract) now frame this as shared responsibility: the report should "allow incidental findings to be mentioned" with "provision to ensure competent and holistic review of all relevant findings," responsibility lying with both reporter and employer. <em>Halligan et al. (Clin Radiol 2026)</em> — reviewed from the <em>medicolegalcourtcase.pdf</em> extract — supplies the empirical context: 41 of 112 abdominal claims (37%) cited incidental pathology, the commonest being missed tumour (23 of 41); only 12 of 41 (29%) were judged substandard. KJY sits on the liability side of that cohort because the hip signs were obvious, not inconspicuous. But where unanticipated pathology is genuinely inconspicuous without a steer from the referrer, <em>Bolam</em> / <em>Hunter v Hanley</em> still protects a reasonable miss — the nuance the 2025 Standards' wording on deficient or misleading referrals is designed to capture. IR(ME)R duties on referrers to supply adequate clinical information reinforce the point. Applies equally in Scotland.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> every plain film is a whole-image examination, not just the referred region. KJY converts the RCR's "holistic review" expectation into a liability rule for obvious incidentals — paediatric hips on abdominal views being the paradigm.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> adopt a systematic search pattern that includes included joints and soft tissues, and report obvious incidentals with a clear recommendation and escalation. Where reporting is outsourced or AI-triaged, confirm the service contract covers non-target review — the 2025 Standards place that duty on the employer too.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> KJY lowers the cost of proving breach where the incidental was obvious, but Halligan bounds it — only 29% of incidental allegations were judged substandard. Test obviousness, not mere presence, and check the referral quality: deficient clinical details are now expressly recognised in the Standards.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="adequate-views" index="12" eyebrow="Adequate views" title="Grimes v Hawkins & Frimley Park Hospital NHS Foundation Trust [2011] EWHC 2004 (QB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: failure to complete adequate radiological views before clearing a trauma patient from spinal precautions.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: following spinal injury, initial cervical spine X-rays failed to image down to the C7/T1 junction, missing an unstable C7 fracture. The patient was discharged from spinal immobilisation on inadequate views.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: the Trust was liable for failing to obtain adequate visualisation of the full cervical spine before lifting precautions. This is a breach at the acquisition stage, not an interpretation dispute: no responsible body of opinion would clear the spine on views that do not demonstrate the cervicothoracic junction. MPS guidance (<em>MPS medicolegal.txt</em> extract) lists "failure to request additional views" — including slipped upper femoral epiphysis examples — among the commonest radiology allegations. Modern practice note: where plain films cannot visualise C7/T1, CT is required (cf. RCR major trauma guidance). Same standard in Scotland under <em>Hunter v Hanley</em>.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> trauma clearance is a protocol decision, not an interpretation debate. Grimes makes the cervicothoracic junction the line between a complete and an incomplete examination — clearing on incomplete views is indefensible regardless of what the visible levels showed.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> hard-stop the pathway: no clearance without C7/T1 visualisation, with CT as the default rescue. Document the junction explicitly ("C7/T1 visualised and aligned") so the record proves completeness.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> Grimes is usually won on the images alone — no expert battle on subtle signs is needed if the junction is absent. Check immobilisation records against image coverage and timing.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="triage-delay" index="13" eyebrow="Triage delay" title="Darnley v Croydon Health Services NHS Trust [2018] UKSC 50">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: breach of duty regarding operational triage, communication of urgency, and delay in arranging critical imaging.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: after a head injury the patient received misleading triage advice about scan wait times, left before an urgent CT, and developed a massive extradural haematoma.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: hospitals owe a duty not to misinform patients in ways that deter them from waiting for urgent, life-saving diagnostic scans. The Supreme Court found breach of duty.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> the scan that is never performed injures as surely as the scan that is misread. Darnley extends the radiology duty beyond the reading room to every staff member whose words determine whether urgent imaging happens.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> agree what front-door staff may say about waits for urgent CT/MRI, audit it, and provide a written escalation route for deteriorating waiters. A radiology service that cannot evidence its triage communications owns the resulting delay.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> Darnley is pleaded as operational breach, not diagnostic error — disclosure should target triage scripts, staffing, and waiting-time records rather than images. It pairs with Shaheen (index 15) on the system-safety theme.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="deferred-mri" index="14" eyebrow="Deferred MRI" title="Jarman v Brighton and Sussex University Hospitals NHS Trust [2021] EWHC 323 (QB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: appropriateness of triage delay for emergency diagnostic imaging — urgent versus immediate MRI.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: a patient presenting to A&E with severe back pain was given an "urgent" MRI within two weeks rather than an immediate emergency scan. The claimant alleged developing cauda equina syndrome required same-day imaging.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: claim dismissed under <em>Bolam</em> (Scottish root: <em>Hunter v Hanley 1955 SC 200</em>). Deferring immediate MRI was acceptable because the patient lacked overt, severe red-flag signs at initial examination, and a responsible body of clinicians would have triaged the same way. Distinguish <em>Darnley</em> (index 13), where the breach was misinformation deterring attendance, and <em>Grimes</em> (index 12), where views obtained were inadequate as a matter of fact. MDU data (<em>article-1789575458816.pdf</em> extract: cauda equina among the commonest delayed-diagnosis allegations) explains why back-pain triage carries disproportionate claims exposure even when defensible.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> "urgent" and "emergency" MRI are different promises, and triage notes are the evidence. Jarman protects a reasoned deferral grounded in contemporaneous red-flag assessment — but only where that assessment is recorded.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> vetting is a clinical act: document which red flags were asked about, what was absent, and what safety-netting was given. Agree vetting criteria with ED and primary care so "urgent within two weeks" has a defensible definition.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> Jarman turns on the examination record, not the images. Test what red flags were documented at triage and whether any responsible body would have deferred — Darnley-style misinformation is a different and easier route to breach.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="unperformed-request" index="15" eyebrow="Unperformed request" title="Shaheen v Daish [2025] EWHC 3288 (KB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: responsibility for follow-through on imaging requisitions — and whether a patient who never attends contributes negligently to the delay.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: a GP issued an electronic walk-in chest X-ray referral without clearly communicating its significance; the scan was never performed, delaying a lung cancer diagnosis. The defence alleged contributory negligence for non-attendance.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: the court rejected contributory negligence — clinicians must clearly inform patients when an unperformed imaging request carries serious diagnostic risks. A walk-in referral issued without explanation does not transfer responsibility to the patient. Consistent with the RCR Standards extract (<em>ppqi_reporting-standards-guidance.pdf</em>): providers must maintain procedures for report acknowledgement, communication of unexpected findings and fail-safe notification. See also <em>Darnley</em> (index 13). Practical consequence: record what the patient was told about the reason for the scan, the timescale, and what to do if it is not performed.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> the unperformed request is a system failure, not a reporting error — and lung cancer is its costliest form. Shaheen moves the duty upstream: the request is not complete when sent, but when the patient understands it and the system confirms completion.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> own the fail-safe end — track unperformed urgent referrals, escalate non-attendance on suspected-cancer pathways, and close the loop back to the referrer rather than assuming attendance.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> contributory negligence will fail where the significance of attendance was never communicated. Disclose the referral trail, the instructions given, and fail-safe logs — attendance records alone will not decide it.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="imaging-causation" index="16" eyebrow="Imaging causation" title="C v North Cumbria University Hospitals NHS Trust [2014] EWHC 61 (QB)">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: medical causation requirements where imaging delays are said to have caused deterioration.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: neurological damage followed a delayed diagnosis in which initial radiological imaging was deferred in favour of clinical observation.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: a claimant must prove on the balance of probabilities that earlier imaging would have altered the definitive outcome, not merely that delay increased the risk of harm. This is orthodox <em>Gregg v Scott [2005] HL</em> territory as summarised in <em>Buchan</em> Ch 7: loss of a chance is not actionable damage in clinical negligence (save the <em>Chester v Afshar [2005] HL</em> consent exception, which does not apply to imaging delay). MDU material (<em>article-1789575458816.pdf</em> extract) confirms causation as a leading defence: claims were discontinued where earlier detection did not alter outcome. Same causation law in Scotland — <em>Gregg</em> binds in both jurisdictions. Scots procedural note: limitation for childhood injury runs from age 16 in Scotland (18 in England and Wales), per the MDU extract.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> breach without causation pays nothing. Most delayed-imaging claims survive or fail on this section, not on the missed finding — the question is always what earlier imaging would have changed, proved with oncology, surgical or neurological evidence.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> when addending a missed finding, record your honest view of whether earlier detection would have altered management — that contemporaneous note often decides causation years later. Avoid overstating ("earlier detection would have cured") or minimising; both will be tested.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> plead causation with a concrete counterfactual — date of earlier diagnosis, stage shift, treatment that would have followed. Loss-of-chance arguments fail outside consent (Gregg); Chester does not rescue an imaging-delay claim.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="catastrophic-quantum" index="17" eyebrow="Catastrophic quantum" title="Sowden v Lodge [2004] EWCA Civ 1370">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: assessment of long-term care needs and quantum of damages after severe radiological diagnostic failure.</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: severe brain injury resulted from delayed emergency radiological intervention and treatment following trauma.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: leading authority on quantifying future care costs and accommodation needs where imaging errors cause catastrophic neurological injury. <em>Buchan</em> Chs 4 and 27 place Sowden in the damages framework: compensation restores the claimant to the pre-negligence position — principally future care, loss of earnings and accommodation — which is why radiology settlements range from ~£4,000 to ~£2 million with most settled cases above £50,000 and costs often exceeding damages (MDU extract: Achilles-tendon consent case settled at £12,500 damages with costs near five times that). Quantum law is materially the same in Scotland, litigated in the Court of Session under the same heads of damage.</p></div>
<div class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-600"><p><strong class="text-navy-900">Why it matters — radiology:</strong> catastrophic brain injury is where a single missed scan becomes a multi-million-pound claim — care, accommodation and loss of earnings dwarf general damages. Sowden explains why early admission and rehabilitation funding decisions matter as much as liability strategy.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For consultant radiologists:</strong> understand that your addendum feeds a quantum machine: care experts will cost lifelong needs from the injury your delay is said to have caused. Accurate, restrained causal language in reports and duty-of-candour letters constrains that exercise honestly.</p><p class="mt-2"><strong class="text-navy-900">For legal professionals:</strong> use Sowden to structure schedules — future care, accommodation (including adapted housing capital costs), and earnings — and note the MDU cost warning: even modest damages carry costs multiples, so Part 36 and early rehabilitation offers carry unusual weight in imaging cases.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="ai-reporting" index="18" eyebrow="AI-assisted reporting" title="AI-Assisted Reporting: Emerging Medicolegal Risk">
<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Issue: who is liable when an AI-assisted report misses or fabricates a finding?</p>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">Facts: AI tools are being deployed across NHS radiology services for triage, structured reporting and decision support. The RCR's 3rd edition Standards (October 2025) require that AI-generated content be clearly identified, that a named human author reviews and approves all AI-derived contributions, and that AI report formats must not imply a degree of scrutiny that has not taken place. No published case law directly addresses AI-assisted reporting error at the time of writing.</p>
<div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-slate-400 py-0.5 pl-5 pr-2"><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Principle: where an AI tool contributes to a report, the named human author bears full responsibility under Bolam. A radiologist who signs an AI-generated report containing a hallucinated finding without independent verification would fall below standard. An AI tool that silently suppresses a finding through training bias creates a different problem: the report may appear complete but omits a material finding. The employer's duty to provide adequate training (per RCR 2025) and to maintain quality assurance extends to AI-assisted workflows. Precautionary principle: document that an AI tool was used, what it generated, and that you independently verified every clinical finding before release.</p></div>
</Section>
<Section id="claim-patterns" index="19" eyebrow="Categories" title="Patterns in radiology claims">
<div class="divide-y divide-slate-200"><div class="py-5"><h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900">System safety</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">Delayed lung cancer on chest radiograph. Breach of fail-safe notification. The duty extends beyond interpretation to effective communication of critical findings. See Shaheen (index 15) and Darnley (index 13).</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900">Fracture and spine</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">Missed fracture. Cauda equina delay. ED misinterpretation. See FB (index 03), Spellman (index 09), Jarman (index 14) and Grimes (index 12). Standard set by post and task.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900">Interventional error</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">Vascular injury. Inadequate consent. Risk disclosure failure. See Montgomery (index 05): the reasonable-patient standard governs material risks for embolisation, biopsy and angioplasty.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900">Abdominal catastrophe</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">Missed bowel perforation or ischaemia on CT. Standard of care, causation and protocol adequacy decide breach and outcome. See Brady (index 08) and White (index 10). Causation requires Gregg analysis (index 16).</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900">Incidental findings</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">37% of abdominal radiology claims cite incidental pathology. Small colonic tumours on unprepared CT. Renal tumours on spine MRI. See Halligan (index 06) and KJY (index 11). The Bolam test, not pure diagnosis, applies.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900">Operational delay</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">Misleading triage advice deterring patients from waiting for urgent imaging. Unperformed walk-in referrals. Inadequate views before clearing trauma. See Darnley (index 13), Shaheen (index 15) and Grimes (index 12).</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="font-bold text-navy-900">AI-assisted error</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">AI hallucination, template bias, silent suppression. The named human author bears full responsibility. Document AI use, verify independently.</p></div></div>
</Section>
<Callout tone="caution" title="Not legal advice">
<p>Case digests here are a reference aid, not advice, and not a substitute for reading the judgment. Citations are given so the primary source can be checked; where a matter turns on the application of a decision to particular facts, take advice from a lawyer instructed on those facts.</p>
</Callout>
